    This lock is suggested in the scenarios where there are high read and less writes.

                        Second Classification of locks: 
                        1) Pessimistic Lock ( shared and exclusive)
                        2) Optimistic Lock  (no lock acquired)
                             Whenever we read a data we maintain a version of that data.
                             While updating the same data, if version mismatches the operation
                             rolls back.



3) Stamped Locks Provides:
        a) Read/ Write lock
        b) Optimistic lock

4) Semaphore Lock: 
    Allows a particular thread to go inside a critical section. But there is a permit(count eg: 2)
    meaning only 2 threads can acquire the lock at the same time. 

        Scenarios: 
        1) There are 2 printers: So we can only allow at max two print request parallely.
        2) Connection Pools: Connection pool defines 5 connections at max.



Inter-thread communication:  
 While using synchronized > it puts a monitored lock > we have wait() and notify() which works.
    So threads can communicate with each other.

With custom locks in picture we cannot use wait() and notify(). That's why we have CONDITIONS: 

await() = wait()
signal() = notify()
